== 4. Results

Each result is stated with its hypotheses, proved, and marked with the verdict reached by its derivation script. Propositions are numbered in the order they were derived.

#thm(kind: [Theorem], number: 1, name: [Existence and value of the first moment])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] $alpha > 0$ and $m > 0$ are fixed, and $X$ has density $f(x) = alpha m^alpha x^(-(alpha+1))$ supported on $[m, oo)$.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] The improper integral $E[X] = integral_m^oo x f(x) dif x$ converges if and only if $alpha > 1$, and then $E[X] = frac(alpha m, alpha - 1)$. For $alpha <= 1$ it diverges to $+oo$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ E[X] = integral_m^oo x f(x) dif x = frac(alpha m, alpha - 1) quad "for" alpha > 1 $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] Write $E[X] = alpha m^alpha integral_m^oo x^(-alpha) dif x$, an improper integral of a power. For $alpha > 1$ the exponent $-(alpha)$ exceeds $-1$, so the antiderivative $x^(1-alpha)/(1-alpha)$ tends to a finite limit at $oo$ and yields $alpha m/(alpha-1)$ after the scale factor is absorbed. For $alpha <= 1$ the integrand fails to decay and the integral diverges. The convergence side of this dichotomy is read off the branch condition of the evaluated improper integral rather than assumed. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] The mean delay is only a meaningful summary statistic above index $1$; below it the first moment does not exist.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Theorem], number: 2, name: [The critical index and the second moment])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] Same hypotheses as Theorem 1.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] $E[X^2] = integral_m^oo x^2 f(x) dif x$ converges if and only if $alpha > 2$, and then $E[X^2] = frac(alpha m^2, alpha - 2)$. Moreover $alpha_c = 2$ is critical: $"Var"(X) = frac(alpha m^2, (alpha-1)^2 (alpha-2))$ is unbounded as $alpha$ decreases to $2$ from above.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ E[X^2] = frac(alpha m^2, alpha - 2), quad "Var"(X) = frac(alpha m^2, (alpha - 1)^2 (alpha - 2)) $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] The integrand $x^2 f(x) = alpha m^alpha x^(1-alpha)$ is a power with exponent $1 - alpha$, which is integrable at infinity exactly when $1 - alpha < -1$, i.e. $alpha > 2$. Evaluating the antiderivative gives the closed form. The critical index then follows from the closed form for the variance, whose denominator carries the factor $(alpha - 2)$; the one-sided limit at $alpha = 2$ is infinite, so the pole is genuine rather than removable. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] $alpha_c = 2$ is the exact boundary of the regime in which a variance-based objective is even defined.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Theorem], number: 3, name: [Closed form for the variance])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] Same hypotheses as Theorem 1, with $alpha > 2$.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] $"Var"(X) = frac(alpha m^2, (alpha - 1)^2 (alpha - 2))$, and this is finite if and only if $alpha > 2$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ "Var"(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2 = frac(alpha m^2, (alpha - 1)^2 (alpha - 2)) $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] Immediate from Theorem 1 and Theorem 2 by the algebraic identity $"Var"(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$. Both moments exist in the regime $alpha > 2$, so the identity applies; combining the two closed forms yields the displayed expression, and its denominator shows finiteness is equivalent to $alpha > 2$. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] Supplies the objective that the routing analysis in the next section minimises.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Theorem], number: 4, name: [No finite-variance objective below the critical index])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] $alpha <= 2$, $m > 0$, and any allocation of work to servers.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] For every $alpha <= 2$ the delay variance is infinite, so no variance-minimising routing policy exists in that regime. The divergence is already present strictly below $alpha_c = 2$ and is logarithmic at $alpha = alpha_c$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ integral_m^oo x^2 f(x) dif x = oo quad "for" alpha <= 2 $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] Fix a sub-critical index, for instance $alpha = 3/2$. The tail of the second-moment integrand is then $x^(-1/2)$, whose antiderivative $2 sqrt(x)$ is unbounded, so the improper integral diverges for every $m > 0$. At the critical index $alpha = 2$ the integrand degenerates to a pure $1/x$ tail, whose antiderivative $2 log(x)$ also diverges, establishing logarithmic rather than power divergence. Since the allocation of work cannot change the law of a single delay, the variance is infinite under every allocation. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] Variance minimisation is well-posed only for $alpha > 2$; below it the policy objective must be replaced by a robust alternative such as a truncated variance, a quantile, or a value-at-risk.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Lemma], number: 5, name: [Load imbalance contributes a non-negative excess])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] Two servers receive integer counts $n_1, n_2 >= 0$ drawn from a fixed total.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] $2(n_1^2 + n_2^2) - (n_1 + n_2)^2 = (n_1 - n_2)^2 >= 0$, with equality if and only if $n_1 = n_2$. More generally, for $m$ servers, $m sum_j n_j^2 - n^2 = sum_(i<j) (n_i - n_j)^2 >= 0$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ m sum_(j=1)^m n_j^2 - n^2 = sum_(1 <= i < j <= m) (n_i - n_j)^2 >= 0 $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] For two servers the identity is the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in its two-point form and is verified by direct expansion. The right-hand side is a sum of squares, so it is non-negative and vanishes exactly when all counts coincide; this is precisely the statement that equal counts minimise the sum of squares at fixed total. Reassigning one task from the fullest server, holding $k_("hi")$, to the emptiest, holding $k_("lo")$, changes the sum of squares by exactly $2 (k_("hi") - k_("lo") - 1)$, which is strictly positive whenever the split is unbalanced by at least two tasks. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] The variance-minimising allocation is the equal split, and the descent direction of the routing policy is the reassignment of a single task from the fullest server to the emptiest.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Theorem], number: 6, name: [The balanced split minimises batch variance])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] A batch of $n$ tasks is split across $m$ servers with integer counts $n_j >= 0$ and $sum_j n_j = n$. Write $n = q m + r$ with $0 <= r < m$.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] Among all such splits, $sum_j n_j^2$ is minimised by the near-equal split $(q+1, q, dots, q, q+1)$ with $r$ servers carrying one extra task, and its exact excess over the Cauchy-Schwarz bound is $r (m - r)$, which vanishes if and only if $m$ divides $n$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ min sum_j n_j^2 = (m - r) q^2 + r (q + 1)^2, quad m min - n^2 = r (m - r) $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] By the preceding lemma the excess $m sum_j n_j^2 - n^2$ is a sum of squares, so the minimiser is characterised by vanishing pairwise differences, that is by equal counts. Over the integers the counts can differ by at most one at a minimum, which is the near-equal split; substituting $n = q m + r$ gives the displayed closed form, and the residual $r (m - r)$ is non-negative and vanishes precisely when $r = 0$. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] Routing policy: assign work so that server loads differ by at most one task. Any deviation is strictly worse, and the penalty is exactly $r (m - r)$ in units of the single-delay variance.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

#thm(kind: [Corollary], number: 7, name: [Exact inversion of the Pareto distribution function])
#indented[
  #text(weight: "bold")[Hypotheses.] Same hypotheses as Theorem 1, with $alpha > 0$, and $p$ in $(0, 1)$.

  #text(weight: "bold")[Statement.] The upper-tail quantile is $Q(p) = m (1 - p)^(-1/alpha)$, and it inverts the distribution exactly: $F(Q(p)) = p$.
  #block(width: 100%, above: 0.5em, below: 0.5em)[$ F(x) = 1 - (m/x)^alpha, quad Q(p) = m (1 - p)^(-1/alpha), quad F(Q(p)) = p $]
  #text(weight: "bold")[Proof.] The Pareto distribution function is $F(x) = 1 - (m/x)^alpha$ on its support. Substituting $Q(p)$ gives $1 - (m / (m (1-p)^(-1/alpha)))^alpha$, whose inner power is a nested radical that must be denested before it simplifies; doing so exactly returns $p$. This is the level at which a truncated-variance or value-at-risk objective must be cut when the variance itself does not exist. #qed

  #text(weight: "bold")[Consequence.] Supplies the cutoff for the robust objective that replaces variance minimisation when $alpha <= alpha_c = 2$.

  #receipt(label: [Verdict], [PROVEN])
]

